# Calendar

Gregorian-calendar helpers, written in pure LOGOS and byte-identical on every tier. Mirrors
Python's `calendar` (isleap / leapdays / monthrange / month_name / day_name). Auto-imported on
reference.

## Note
True if `year` is a Gregorian leap year: divisible by 4, except centuries, unless divisible by 400.

## To isLeapYear (year: Int) -> Bool:
    If year mod 400 is equal to 0:
        Return true.
    If year mod 100 is equal to 0:
        Return false.
    If year mod 4 is equal to 0:
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Number of days in `month` of `year` (28 or 29 for February per `isLeapYear`, 30 or 31 otherwise).

## To daysInMonth (year: Int) and (month: Int) -> Int:
    If month is equal to 2:
        If isLeapYear(year):
            Return 29.
        Return 28.
    If month is equal to 4:
        Return 30.
    If month is equal to 6:
        Return 30.
    If month is equal to 9:
        Return 30.
    If month is equal to 11:
        Return 30.
    Return 31.

## Note
Counts the leap years in the half-open range `[y1, y2)`: the excess of the elapsed days over 365 per year, from `makeMoment` (O(1), no per-year loop).

## To leapDaysBetween (y1: Int) and (y2: Int) -> Int:
    If y2 is at most y1:
        Return 0.
    Let a be makeMoment(y1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let b be makeMoment(y2, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let seconds be the seconds between a and b.
    Let days be seconds / 86400.
    Return days - (365 * (y2 - y1)).

## Note
The full English month name for `month` 1-12 (e.g. 1 -> "January", 12 -> "December").

## To monthName (month: Int) -> Text:
    If month is equal to 1:
        Return "January".
    If month is equal to 2:
        Return "February".
    If month is equal to 3:
        Return "March".
    If month is equal to 4:
        Return "April".
    If month is equal to 5:
        Return "May".
    If month is equal to 6:
        Return "June".
    If month is equal to 7:
        Return "July".
    If month is equal to 8:
        Return "August".
    If month is equal to 9:
        Return "September".
    If month is equal to 10:
        Return "October".
    If month is equal to 11:
        Return "November".
    Return "December".

## Note
The full English weekday name for `weekday` 1-7, ISO 8601: 1 is Monday and 7 is Sunday — the same convention as the `the weekday of <date>` built-in. Out of range (not 1-7) answers "".

## To dayName (weekday: Int) -> Text:
    If weekday is equal to 1:
        Return "Monday".
    If weekday is equal to 2:
        Return "Tuesday".
    If weekday is equal to 3:
        Return "Wednesday".
    If weekday is equal to 4:
        Return "Thursday".
    If weekday is equal to 5:
        Return "Friday".
    If weekday is equal to 6:
        Return "Saturday".
    If weekday is equal to 7:
        Return "Sunday".
    Return "".

## Note
The three-letter English month abbreviation for `month` 1-12 (e.g. "Jan"), or "" if out of range.

## To monthAbbr (month: Int) -> Text:
    If month is equal to 1:
        Return "Jan".
    If month is equal to 2:
        Return "Feb".
    If month is equal to 3:
        Return "Mar".
    If month is equal to 4:
        Return "Apr".
    If month is equal to 5:
        Return "May".
    If month is equal to 6:
        Return "Jun".
    If month is equal to 7:
        Return "Jul".
    If month is equal to 8:
        Return "Aug".
    If month is equal to 9:
        Return "Sep".
    If month is equal to 10:
        Return "Oct".
    If month is equal to 11:
        Return "Nov".
    If month is equal to 12:
        Return "Dec".
    Return "".

## Note
The three-letter English weekday abbreviation for `weekday` 1-7, ISO 8601: 1 is "Mon" and 7 is "Sun" — the same convention as the `the weekday of <date>` built-in. Out of range (not 1-7) answers "".

## To dayAbbr (weekday: Int) -> Text:
    If weekday is equal to 1:
        Return "Mon".
    If weekday is equal to 2:
        Return "Tue".
    If weekday is equal to 3:
        Return "Wed".
    If weekday is equal to 4:
        Return "Thu".
    If weekday is equal to 5:
        Return "Fri".
    If weekday is equal to 6:
        Return "Sat".
    If weekday is equal to 7:
        Return "Sun".
    Return "".

## Note
Signed day count from 1970-01-01 to `year`-01-01 (negative before 1970): the elapsed days between the two `makeMoment` instants (O(1), no per-year loop).

## To daysBeforeYear (year: Int) -> Int:
    Let epoch be makeMoment(1970, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let startOfYear be makeMoment(year, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let seconds be the seconds between epoch and startOfYear.
    Return seconds / 86400.

## Note
Days elapsed from the start of `year` to the first of `month`: the elapsed days between the two `makeMoment` instants (O(1), no per-month loop).

## To daysBeforeMonth (year: Int) and (month: Int) -> Int:
    Let startOfYear be makeMoment(year, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let startOfMonth be makeMoment(year, month, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let seconds be the seconds between startOfYear and startOfMonth.
    Return seconds / 86400.

## Note
Converts a UTC civil date-time to Unix seconds since 1970-01-01: the elapsed seconds between the epoch and the instant, both built with the `makeMoment` built-in (O(1), no per-year loop).

## To timegm (year: Int) and (month: Int) and (day: Int) and (hour: Int) and (minute: Int) and (second: Int) -> Int:
    Let epoch be makeMoment(1970, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0).
    Let instant be makeMoment(year, month, day, hour, minute, second).
    Return the seconds between epoch and instant.

## Note
The 1-based day of the year (1 for January 1; 365 or 366 for December 31), as `daysBeforeMonth` plus the day.

## To dayOfYear (year: Int) and (month: Int) and (day: Int) -> Int:
    Return daysBeforeMonth(year, month) + day.
